 (MIMic): n=20. ORR: 65%. mPFS: 29.6 months. mOS: 52.8 months.\n        *   P0589 (FMT-LUMINate): n=20. ORR: 70% (2 CR, 12 PR). Grade >3 irAEs: 65%.\n        *   I will format these strictly as requested.\n    *   *Usable Content:* Synthesize the mechanistic insights and clinical context from the materials, while clearly stating the limitation regarding Phase III RCTs. Focus on how the materials address the user scope (mechanism of microbiome modulation of ICI efficacy) and the specific question.\n        *   Mechanisms discussed: Microbiota engraftment is often transient; durable engraftment linked to clinical benefit. Baseline myeloid-dominant environment (high CD14+ monocytes) and high IL-8 associate with poor outcomes, while activated T-cell signatures (e.g., granzyme B+ CD8+ T cells, differentiated CD4+ EM/Th1, Tregs, ICOS+ CD8 T cells) favor better PFS/OS. These immune features reflect host disease state/ICI exposure rather than being directly modified by FMT to improve outcomes. Donor selection (ICI responders vs healthy), patient heterogeneity (rapidly progressive vs stable), and timing (ICI-refractory salvage vs first-line) heavily influence efficacy. Mild FMT-specific GI toxicity may correlate with successful microbial integration. SCFA-producing taxa (Blautia, Roseburia, Faecalibacterium) enriched in responders.\n        *   Address the question: The provided materials consist exclusively of Phase I/IIa/II single-arm or basket trials. They do not contain data from completed Phase III RCTs published in 2024-2025. One trial (TACITO) is noted as recently published randomized data, but its Phase III status and survival outcomes are not detailed here. Thus, the current evidence base described remains at the early-phase exploratory stage, highlighting the need for large-scale randomized validation.\n        *   Structure usable_content: Start with direct findings/mechanisms from the studies, note the discrepancy in settings (salvage vs first-line, donor types),